Produce one complete game-character reference sprite in a relaxed player-avatar standing pose.

Subject: a knight.

Feature audit before drawing (do this internally, then render): identify and preserve the subject's hairstyle, hair color, eye color, outfit layers, accessories, weapon or signature prop, symbolic motifs, and dominant colors.

Render contract (obey strictly): true low-resolution pixel-art game sprite, like a 32-64px sprite enlarged on the canvas, chunky readable silhouette, clean dark 1px outline, visible square pixel blocks, grid-aligned hard pixel edges, limited shared palette, solid tone clusters, flat color shading with at most one highlight step and one shadow step, simple readable face and clearly separated limbs. Never use painterly rendering, smooth gradients, airbrush shading, glossy lighting, anti-aliased fine detail, high-definition pixel art, fine-grained pixel art, anime illustration, concept art, or 3D rendering.

Game-sprite design contract:
- Interpret the subject as a game-ready character sprite, not an illustration, poster, sticker, mascot logo, or concept-art render.
- Preserve the subject's identity through a strong silhouette, hairstyle, outfit shapes, accessories, weapon or signature prop, and dominant color blocks.
- Simplify anatomy into readable sprite shapes: compact torso, clear head shape, simple arms and legs, minimal joint detail, no tiny anatomy rendering.
- Hair, clothing layers, capes, hats, weapons and accessories should read as distinct hard-edged pixel shapes, not detailed painted textures.
- Keep the face simple at sprite scale: readable eyes and mouth, minimal facial detail, no realistic nose or painted skin texture.

Pixel rendering contract:
- The image must look like a 32-64px game sprite enlarged to the canvas, not newly painted at high resolution.
- Use chunky square pixel blocks, clean 1px outline, solid tone clusters, limited palette, minimal two-step flat shading.
- No dithering, no smooth gradients, no soft shadow, no blur, no airbrush, no texture, no fine hair strands, no tiny jewelry detail that would vanish at 64px.
- Every important shape must remain readable when shrunk to a thumbnail: silhouette first, details second.

Framing:
- A single figure, head to feet, vertically centered, occupying about three quarters of the canvas height with generous breathing room on every side.
- Idle standing sprite pose: feet level, weight balanced, arms relaxed but readable.
- Almost flat 2D game-sprite view; avoid dramatic perspective, foreshortening, cinematic camera angles, and illustration-style posing.
- One continuous silhouette — nothing detached, no trailing accessories or particles.

Keying canvas (the renderer mattes this away — obey exactly):
- Fill the ENTIRE background, edge to edge, with pure keying magenta (#FF00FF), perfectly uniform edge to edge — a single flat color touching all four image borders. No gradient, texture, scenery, floor, panel, frame, or border of any kind.
- The subject must avoid magenta, pink and purple entirely — clothing, props, highlights and effects included — so the keyer never eats part of the character.
- Drop every shadow and contact patch; the ground is implied, never painted.

Reject (these break automatic extraction):
- ANY frame, border, or decoration around the image or around a pose: no film strip, no sprocket holes or perforations, no photo/polaroid frame, no panel dividers, no outline box, no vignette. The background reaches every edge unbroken.
- Motion garnish — streaks, speed lines, blur, after-images, arcs, swooshes, trails.
- Free-floating bits — sparkles, stars, dust, smoke puffs, icons, symbols, or any mark not fused to the body.
- Text, numbers, captions, grids, rulers, speech or thought bubbles, UI, watermarks.
- Any pose that is clipped by the edge, or whose pixels bridge into the neighbouring pose.
